title: 1. Why four questions, and why most requests go unplanned
pid: 270e71e3-b42d-4e1d-919b-137ded5d3b57
body: |2-
  A plan that would only say "this is how we will build it" is not
  worth writing. Minds that work on one change one after another each
  make choices the others cannot see, and the choices conflict. A mind
  that tries to finish a large thing in one session runs out of room
  and leaves it half done. Most requests answer no to all four, and
  planning those is the costly error; an unplanned request still gets
  a mission that says what done looks like, and a review (step 8). The
  appetite is a budget, not an estimate: the plan shapes the work to
  fit it. The four answers are also the request's first signs (step 4).
  Rests on: read (Google's design docs; Cognition; Bezos's one-way
  doors; Rust's "substantial" changes; Anthropic's long-running
  harnesses; Anthropic's research system, whose early versions spent
  fifty agents on simple questions; Shape Up's appetite). The four
  questions as a set are ours, and the threshold is unsure.
